34 (118th):** her cosponsor line and the bill text, plus her Yea on Roll Call 192. The vote was 219–203, so it is well clear of the near-unanimous limit.\n- **H.R. 28 (119th):** same structure, plus her Yea on Roll Call 12, 218–206.\n\nBoth bills deal only with athletics. They make it a Title IX violation to let \"a person whose sex is male\" (defined by sex at birth) join a program \"designated for women or girls\". Other teams stay open, which rules out rung 5, a complete ban from sport. Both bills also order a study, but the operative section is what decides the coding.\n\n**Op-ed (2024-02-27):** I coded it as her own words, `statement-other`. It shows which side she is on but does not by itself fit one rung exactly (`direction-only`), so it is not in `rests_on`. One quote from it is tagged `direction` and marked quotable.\n\n**`needs_source`:** in the op-ed she says she cosponsors the Protection of Women in Olympic and Amateur Sports Act. No record of that bill was among the sources, so I listed it there.","ttft_ms":13350,"type":"result","duration_ms":39650,"uuid":"4712cb56-c170-4d58-85f5-fe055eb75d52","ttft_stream_ms":1926,"time_to_request_ms":263,"first_content_frame_ms":1926,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
